Support nested morphism applications

Allow an enclosing model morphism to act on members at any nesting depth
through paths such as `f.X.T`. Explain path resolution and application
population with surface syntax examples.

Infer nested application arguments and results from the morphism type,
including their distinct parent chains. Reuse inferred parents across
statements and later annotations without hiding concrete type conflicts.
Exercise these cases through evaluation and compile-error integration
tests, including missing codomains and parent images in conclusions.

Reject invalid nested morphism applications through the Rust API before
inserting them. Check the source membership and the full mapped parent
chain, while accepting any valid membership of a shared element. Cover
invalid insertions with expected-panic evaluation integration tests.

Name the morphism and member types in each application so their roles
are explicit throughout signature lookup and code generation.

Keep complex enum checks exhaustive so new variants require an explicit
decision, while allowing concise Option handling. Document this rule
and the preference for fewer branches and shallow nesting in AGENTS.md.
